# Driving A tart macOS VM Over VNC

`tart` wraps Virtualization.framework, so a clean macOS guest is
`tart clone ghcr.io/cirruslabs/macos-sequoia-base:latest` plus
`tart run <name> --vnc-experimental --dir=vmshare:<hostdir>`. Learned the hard
way during the 2026-08-08 acceptance test of this repo's installer.

**Host↔guest transport:**

- The Cirrus base image ships with Remote Login **off**, and even after turning
  it on, host→guest SSH from an agent shell is blocked by macOS Local Network
  Privacy. Do not budget time for SSH. Use `--dir=` (virtiofs, mounts at
  `/Volumes/My Shared Files/<tag>`) for both scripts and logs, and drive
  commands by typing into Terminal over VNC.
- `--no-graphics` guests can fail to finish booting; `--vnc-experimental`
  attaches a display and boots reliably. The VNC URL and one-time password are
  printed on stdout, so tee that output to a file.
- Apple's framework caps concurrent VMs at 2. A second agent running its own VM
  will make yours fail to start; use a unique VM name.

**VNC input quirks (vncdotool):**

- The framebuffer is HiDPI. A 1024x768 screenshot is a 2048x1536 framebuffer,
  so every click coordinate is **2x the pixel you measured on the screenshot**.
  Un-doubled clicks land near (0,0) and open the Apple menu.
- `type` sends unshifted characters only. No uppercase, no `:`, no `|`. Keep
  typed commands all-lowercase (`/volumes/my shared files/...` works, the path
  is case-insensitive) and put anything else in a script on the shared folder.
  Environment variables must be exported inside that script.
- The Command key is not delivered. `key cmd-n` hangs the client, `key super-n`
  does nothing. Use the menu bar with the pointer instead (Shell > New Window >
  Basic) when you need a second shell.
- Security-sensitive dialogs ("Terminal would like to administer your
  computer", the Determinate Nix keychain prompt) accept a real VNC pointer
  click on Allow once coordinates are correct, but never synthetic keystrokes.

**Guest prep for a fresh-Mac fidelity test:** the base image has passwordless
sudo, so move the `NOPASSWD` drop-in aside or the sudo behaviour under test is
fake. Create the account the flake expects with `dscl` (`sysadminctl` raises the
system-modal TCC dialog), add it to `admin` with `dseditgroup`, then
`createhomedir -c -u`, and pre-seed
`~/Library/Preferences/com.apple.SetupAssistant.plist` so the first login is a
plain desktop.
